  <section id="measure" aria-labelledby="measure-h">
    <h2 id="measure-h">How to Measure Your Stride Length</h2>
    <ol class="tp-steps"><li><strong>Mark a distance.</strong> Measure 100 feet (or 20 meters) on a track, sidewalk or long hallway.</li><li><strong>Walk it normally.</strong> Start walking a few steps before the line so you are at your normal pace, and count the steps between the marks.</li><li><strong>Divide.</strong> Divide the distance by the number of steps to get step length. Repeat twice and average.</li></ol>
  </section>

  <section id="pedometer" aria-labelledby="pedometer-h">
    <h2 id="pedometer-h">Setting a Pedometer or Fitness Tracker</h2>
    <p>Enter your measured step length into your tracker for better distance estimates; height-based guesses can be off by 10% or more. Running steps are much longer than walking steps, so trackers that let you set both will be more accurate. To turn a step count into miles, use the <a href="/steps-to-distance-calculator/">steps to distance calculator</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
    <h2 id="faq-h">Stride Length FAQ</h2>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is the average stride length?</h3></summary><p>The average walking step is about 2.1 to 2.5 feet (64–76 cm) for adults, so an average stride of two steps is about 4.2 to 5 feet.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How do I calculate stride length from height?</h3></summary><p>Multiply your height by 0.415 (men) or 0.413 (women) for step length. For a 70 inch tall man: 70 × 0.415 = 29 inches.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How many steps are in a mile?</h3></summary><p>About 2,000 to 2,500 walking steps, depending on height and pace. Running, it is about 1,400 to 1,700.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Is stride length the same as step length?</h3></summary><p>No. A stride is two steps. Many devices use the word stride to mean a single step.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Does a longer stride make you faster?</h3></summary><p>Partly, but overstriding wastes energy. Most runners speed up best by slightly increasing cadence (steps per minute) rather than reaching further with each step.</p></details>
  </section>

